<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Root LaunchDaemon for kanata, derived from kanata v1.12.0
  cfg_samples/kanata.plist. Root is required because the Karabiner
  VirtualHIDDevice daemon exposes its IPC in
  "/Library/Application Support/org.pqrs/tmp/rootonly" (mode 700, owner root);
  home-manager's launchd.agents writes a user agent and cannot do that.

  config/darwin.nix substitutes @KANATA_CFG@ with the keymap's absolute path
  and writes the result to ~/.config/kanata/dev.kanata.kanata.plist, because a
  daemon running as root does not resolve "~" to this user's home.

  The binary path is fixed on purpose. /usr/local/libexec/kanata is a copy of
  the profile binary, never a store or profile path, so a "home-manager switch"
  cannot change the executable behind an existing TCC grant. A stable symlink
  under $HOME was rejected: a root daemon executing a user writable path is a
  local privilege escalation surface.

  macOS prerequisites. All of them are manual; nix does none of this:

  1. Driver v6.2.0. kanata's karabiner IPC is pinned to that release and newer
     minors are not guaranteed. Install the pkg from
     https://github.com/pqrs-org/Karabiner-DriverKit-VirtualHIDDevice/releases/tag/v6.2.0
     then force activate and approve the system extension:
       sudo /Applications/.Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Manager.app/Contents/MacOS/Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Manager forceActivate
     System Settings > General > Login Items & Extensions > Driver Extensions
     must list org.pqrs.Karabiner-DriverKit-VirtualHIDDevice as enabled.

  2. VHID daemon. The standalone pkg does not start it. With Karabiner-Elements
     installed, KE manages the daemon itself and this step is skipped. Without
     KE, install kanata's cfg_samples/karabiner-vhid-daemon.plist as
     /Library/LaunchDaemons/org.pqrs.Karabiner-VirtualHIDDevice-Daemon.plist and
     "sudo launchctl bootstrap system" it. A missing daemon shows up as a
     repeating "connect_failed asio.system:2" in /var/log/kanata.log.

  3. Stage the binary. The daemon runs this copy, never a store or profile
     path, so TCC attaches to a path that home-manager switch does not change:
       sudo install -d -m 0755 /usr/local/libexec
       sudo install -m 0755 "$(command -v kanata)" /usr/local/libexec/kanata

  4. Permissions. A root daemon cannot raise a TCC prompt, so establish them
     from an interactive run of the staged path first, so the grant lands on
     the executable the daemon actually runs:
       sudo /usr/local/libexec/kanata -c @KANATA_CFG@
     Input Monitoring is required to read the keyboard. Accessibility is also
     required for the grab through this driver, not only for mouse events.
     ctrl+space+esc ends that run. The macos-request-permissions flag
     (same binary, flag instead of -c) only registers the Accessibility entry.

  5. Install the daemon:
       sudo install -m 0644 -o root -g wheel \
         ~/.config/kanata/dev.kanata.kanata.plist /Library/LaunchDaemons/
       sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/dev.kanata.kanata.plist
     After a keymap edit only the job needs a restart (alias ksa). After an
     edit to this plist: bootout, reinstall, bootstrap. Logs: /var/log/kanata.log

  The keymap the daemon reads is owned by dit and is a symlink into the
  dotfiles repo, so it is user writable while the daemon is root. That is only
  safe because the staged binary is pkgs.kanata, built without the cmd feature:
  a cmd action cannot run even if the keymap asks for it. Never stage
  `pkgs.kanata.override { withCmd = true; }` here, and keep danger-enable-cmd
  and (cmd ...) out of the keymap.

  Re-copying the binary on a kanata upgrade can invalidate Input Monitoring.
  If remapping stops, toggle kanata off and on in that pane.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>dev.kanata.kanata</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/libexec/kanata</string>
    <string>--cfg</string>
    <string>@KANATA_CFG@</string>
  </array>
  <key>UserName</key>
  <string>root</string>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/var/log/kanata.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/var/log/kanata.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
